**Requirements**
- Render a list of 100,000 items (e.g. 10k contacts) with fixed row height (e.g. 48px) inside a scrollable container of height 400px.
- Only mount DOM nodes for visible rows + overscan (e.g. 5 items above/below).
- Support dynamic data, scrollToIndex(index), and keyboard navigation (↑/↓).
- Show a scrollbar that reflects the full list height.

**Architecture**
```
outer div (overflow:auto, height:400) -> inner spacer (height = itemCount * rowHeight)
                                        -> absolute positioned visible slice (top = startIndex * rowHeight)
```
- Compute `startIndex = floor(scrollTop / rowHeight)`, `visibleCount = ceil(containerHeight / rowHeight) + overscan*2`.
- Slice `items.slice(startIndex, endIndex)` and render with `transform: translateY(offset)`.
- Or use `react-window` `FixedSizeList` — same math under the hood with `itemSize` + `itemCount`.

**What interviewers look for:** windowing math, spacer vs translate, overscan trade-off, memoizing row (`React.memo`), avoiding re-render of all rows on scroll, `useRef` for scrollTop, handling `scrollToIndex`.

**Reference implementation (gold)**
```jsx
import { FixedSizeList as List } from "react-window";

function Row({ index, style, data }) {
  const item = data[index];
  return <div style={style} className="row">{item.name}</div>;
}

export default function VirtualizedList({ items }) {
  return (
    <List height={400} itemCount={items.length} itemSize={48} width="100%" itemData={items} overscanCount={5}>
      {Row}
    </List>
  );
}
// Vanilla fallback: measure scrollTop, compute slice, render via translateY.
// Cleanup: throttle scroll handler with rAF.
```

*Why trending 2025-26:* TanStack Virtual / virtuoso replaced react-window in many stacks; interviewers now ask variable-height + sticky headers.

**Requirements**
- Same 100k list but each row has variable height (text + optional image). Container height 600px.
- Measure each rendered row with `ResizeObserver` and cache heights in `Map<index, height>`.
- Estimate unmeasured rows (e.g. 80px avg) and correct scrollbar as measurements arrive.
- Support `VariableSizeList` from react-window or `react-virtuoso` out-of-the-box.

**Architecture**
- `heightCache` + `estimatedHeight`. `getItemSize(i) => cache.get(i) ?? estimated`.
- Cumulative offsets via prefix sum; binary search to find startIndex from scrollTop.
- On `ResizeObserver` callback, update cache and recompute total height; if delta, adjust scroll.

**What interviewers look for:** dynamic measurement, cache invalidation, binary search over offsets, jank-free correction, `AutoSizer` to fill parent.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
import { VariableSizeList } from "react-window";
import AutoSizer from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function useHeightCache(estimated = 80) {
  const cache = React.useRef(new Map());
  const getSize = (i) => cache.current.get(i) ?? estimated;
  const setSize = (i, h) => cache.current.set(i, h);
  return { getSize, setSize };
}

export function VariableFeed({ items }) {
  const listRef = React.useRef();
  const { getSize, setSize } = useHeightCache();
  const Row = ({ index, style }) => {
    const ref = React.useRef();
    React.useEffect(() => {
      const ro = new ResizeObserver(([e]) => {
        setSize(index, e.contentRect.height);
        listRef.current?.resetAfterIndex(index);
      });
      if (ref.current) ro.observe(ref.current);
      return () => ro.disconnect();
    }, [index]);
    return <div style={style}><div ref={ref}>{items[index].content}</div></div>;
  };
  return (
    <AutoSizer>{({ height, width }) => (
      <VariableSizeList ref={listRef} height={height} width={width} itemCount={items.length} itemSize={getSize} estimatedItemSize={80}>
        {Row}
      </VariableSizeList>
    )}</AutoSizer>
  );
}
```

*Trending:* All 2025 feeds (X/Twitter, LinkedIn clones) require this; interviewers test ResizeObserver + virtuoso.

**Requirements**
- Render a tree from JSON: `{ id, name, type: "file"|"folder", children? }` with unlimited nesting.
- Expand/collapse folders with animation; show indentation guides and icons.
- Drag any file/folder to reorder or move into another folder (including nesting). Prevent dropping a folder into itself/descendant.
- Support rename (double-click), delete, new file/folder, and keyboard nav (↑/↓/Enter).

**Architecture**
- Normalize tree to keep O(1) lookup: `Map<id, node>` + `parentMap`.
- DnD via HTML5 `draggable` or `@dnd-kit` / `react-arborist`. On `dragover`, compute drop position (inside / before / after) via pointer Y vs row middle.
- Immutable tree update: remove node from old parent, insert into new parent at index; guard `isDescendant(dragId, dropId)`.

**What interviewers look for:** recursive tree rendering, normalized state, drag payload (`dataTransfer`), drop indicators, cycle prevention, accessibility.

**Reference implementation (core logic)**
```jsx
function moveNode(tree, dragId, targetId, position) {
  if (dragId === targetId || isDescendant(tree, dragId, targetId)) return tree;
  const without = removeNode(tree, dragId);
  return insertNode(without, dragId, targetId, position);
}

function FileNode({ node, depth, onDrop }) {
  const [isOver, setIsOver] = React.useState(false);
  return (
    <div
      draggable
      onDragStart={e => e.dataTransfer.setData("text/plain", node.id)}
      onDragOver={e => { e.preventDefault(); setIsOver(true); }}
      onDragLeave={() => setIsOver(false)}
      onDrop={e => { onDrop(e.dataTransfer.getData("text/plain"), node.id); setIsOver(false); }}
      style={{ paddingLeft: depth * 16, background: isOver ? "#e0f2ff" : "" }}
    >
      {node.type === "folder" ? "📁" : "📄"} {node.name}
      {node.children?.map(c => <FileNode key={c.id} node={c} depth={depth+1} onDrop={onDrop} />)}
    </div>
  );
}
```

*Trending 2025:* Every frontend onsite now asks tree DnD (Notion, VS Code, Jira); @dnd-kit successor to react-beautiful-dnd.

**Requirements**
- Breadcrumbs showing path to current folder; click to navigate.
- Live search that filters tree while preserving ancestors of matches (highlight matches).
- Right-click context menu (New File, New Folder, Rename, Delete) positioned via portal; close on outside click/Esc.

**Architecture**
- Derive `currentPath` by walking `parentMap` from selected id to root.
- Filter: DFS, keep node if itself matches or any descendant matches; return pruned tree.
- Context menu: `onContextMenu` preventDefault, store `{x,y, nodeId}`, render in `createPortal`, use `getBoundingClientRect` + flip if near viewport edge.

**What interviewers look for:** path derivation, filtered tree with ancestor preservation, portal + positioning, outside-click.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
function filterTree(nodes, query) {
  if (!query) return nodes;
  return nodes.flatMap(n => {
    const match = n.name.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase());
    if (n.children) {
      const filtered = filterTree(n.children, query);
      if (filtered.length) return [{ ...n, children: filtered, expanded: true }];
    }
    return match ? [n] : [];
  });
}

// Context menu portal
function ContextMenu({ x, y, onAction, onClose }) {
  const ref = React.useRef();
  React.useEffect(() => {
    const h = e => !ref.current.contains(e.target) && onClose();
    document.addEventListener("mousedown", h); return () => document.removeEventListener("mousedown", h);
  }, []);
  return ReactDOM.createPortal(
    <div ref={ref} style={{ position: "fixed", left: x, top: y }} role="menu">
      <button onClick={() => onAction("rename")}>Rename</button>
      <button onClick={() => onAction("delete")}>Delete</button>
    </div>, document.body);
}
```

*Trending:* Adds polish interviewers expect beyond basic tree in 2025.

**Requirements**
- Columns (To Do / Doing / Done) with cards; drag cards between columns and reorder within a column.
- Add/edit/delete cards, add columns, persist to localStorage.
- Real-time sync: use Yjs (`yjs` + `y-websocket` or `y-webrtc`) so two tabs/users see live updates without conflicts (CRDT).

**Architecture**
- Model as `Y.Map` for board + `Y.Array` per column. Example: `yDoc.getMap("board")`, `yArray.push([{id, title}])`.
- Bind Yjs to React via `useYjs` hook: observe `yArray.observe(() => setCards(yArray.toArray()))`.
- DnD with `@dnd-kit` `SortableContext` per column; on `dragEnd`, do `yArray.delete(oldIdx,1); yArray.insert(newIdx,[moved])` — Yjs handles merge.

**What interviewers look for:** CRDT mental model (no central lock), Yjs doc/provider setup, mapping Y types to React state, conflict-free drag.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
import * as Y from "yjs";
import { WebrtcProvider } from "y-webrtc";

function useKanban(room = "kanban-demo") {
  const [doc] = React.useState(() => new Y.Doc());
  const yColumns = doc.getArray("columns");
  React.useEffect(() => {
    const provider = new WebrtcProvider(room, doc);
    if (yColumns.length === 0) {
      yColumns.push([{ id: "todo", title: "To Do", cards: new Y.Array() }]);
    }
    return () => provider.destroy();
  }, []);
  const moveCard = (fromCol, fromIdx, toCol, toIdx) => {
    doc.transact(() => {
      const card = fromCol.cards.get(fromIdx);
      fromCol.cards.delete(fromIdx, 1);
      toCol.cards.insert(toIdx, [card]);
    });
  };
  return { yColumns, moveCard };
}

// Render: <DndContext onDragEnd={handle}><SortableContext />...
```

*Trending 2025-26:* Figma/Notion-style CRDT boards replaced plain DnD boards in interviews.

**Requirements**
- Columns with WIP limits (e.g. Doing max 3); show count badge red when exceeded.
- Swimlanes grouping cards by assignee; drag across columns and lanes.
- Card modal with description, assignee, labels, due date; optimistic update.

**Architecture**
- State: `columns: {id, title, wipLimit, cardIds[]} ` + `cards: Map<id, card>` + `swimlaneKey`.
- On drop, check `targetColumn.cardIds.length >= wipLimit ? reject + shake animation : allow`.
- Render swimlane rows: `lanes.map(lane => columns.map(col => filter(col.cards, lane)))`. Use CSS grid.

**What interviewers look for:** WIP validation before commit, lane grouping derivation, nested DnD contexts, optimistic UI.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
function canMove(cards, targetCol, wipLimit) {
  return wipLimit == null || cards.filter(c => c.columnId === targetCol.id).length < wipLimit;
}

function Kanban({ columns, cards, onMove }) {
  const handleDragEnd = ({ active, over }) => {
    const card = cards.find(c => c.id === active.id);
    const targetCol = columns.find(c => c.id === over.id);
    if (!canMove(cards, targetCol, targetCol.wipLimit)) {
      toast.error(`WIP limit ${targetCol.wipLimit} reached`);
      return;
    }
    onMove(card.id, targetCol.id);
  };
  return (
    <DndContext onDragEnd={handleDragEnd}>
      <div className="board" style={{ display: "grid", gridTemplateColumns: `repeat(${columns.length}, 280)` }}>
        {columns.map(col => <Column key={col.id} col={col} cards={cards.filter(c => c.columnId===col.id)} />)}
      </div>
    </DndContext>
  );
}
```

*Gold detail:* Add `aria-label` for WIP, drag handle, and column roles.

**Requirements**
- Trigger with `⌘K` / `Ctrl+K` and `/`; overlay with input + grouped results (Actions, Files, Recent).
- Fuzzy search (e.g. `fuse.js` or `cmdk` built-in) ranking; highlight matched chars.
- Keyboard: ↑/↓ to navigate, Enter to execute, Esc to close; show `⌘` shortcuts; trap focus.

**Architecture**
- Use `cmdk` (`<Command><Command.Input><Command.List>`) or `kbar` (`KBarProvider` + `KBarPortal`). Both handle scoring + a11y.
- Register actions: `{ id, name, keywords, shortcut, perform }`. Filter via `commandScore`.
- Global hotkey: `useEffect(() => { const h=e=> (e.metaKey||e.ctrlKey)&&e.key==="k" && (e.preventDefault(), setOpen(o=>!o)); window.addEventListener("keydown",h); return ()=>remove... })`.

**What interviewers look for:** global hotkey, portal + focus trap, fuzzy ranking, grouping, action registry pattern, Esc/outside-click.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
import { Command } from "cmdk";

export function Palette({ open, setOpen, actions }) {
  React.useEffect(() => {
    const down = e => { if (e.key==="k" && (e.metaKey||e.ctrlKey)) { e.preventDefault(); setOpen(o=>!o); } };
    document.addEventListener("keydown", down); return () => document.removeEventListener("keydown", down);
  }, []);
  return (
    <Command.Dialog open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen} label="Command menu">
      <Command.Input placeholder="Type a command or search..." />
      <Command.List>
        <Command.Empty>No results.</Command.Empty>
        <Command.Group heading="Actions">
          {actions.map(a => (
            <Command.Item key={a.id} onSelect={a.perform} keywords={a.keywords}>
              {a.name} <span className="shortcut">{a.shortcut}</span>
            </Command.Item>
          ))}
        </Command.Group>
      </Command.List>
    </Command.Dialog>
  );
}
```

*Trending:* cmdk + kbar are standard in Linear, Vercel, shadcn apps — asked in 70% of 2025 frontend onsites.

**Requirements**
- Two panels side-by-side with a draggable divider (8px handle).
- Drag to resize; respect `min`/`max` widths (e.g. 200–600px); persist to localStorage.
- Handle supports mouse, touch, and keyboard (ArrowLeft/Right 10px).
- Hook API: `const { size, handleProps } = useResizable({ initial: 320, min: 200, max: 600 })`.

**Architecture**
- `useResizable` stores `size` in state; on `pointerdown`, capture pointer, add `pointermove` listener on window to compute `newSize = startSize + (clientX - startX)`, clamp, update.
- Use `pointer` events (not mouse) for touch+pen; set `touch-action: none`.
- Persist: `useEffect(() => localStorage.setItem(key, size), [size])`.

**What interviewers look for:** pointer capture, window-level move listener, clamp, rAF throttling, keyboard a11y, reusable hook design.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
function useResizable({ initial = 300, min = 200, max = 600, storageKey } = {}) {
  const [size, setSize] = React.useState(() => {
    const v = storageKey && localStorage.getItem(storageKey);
    return v ? Number(v) : initial;
  });
  const startRef = React.useRef(null);
  const onPointerDown = e => {
    e.currentTarget.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
    startRef.current = { x: e.clientX, size };
    const onMove = ev => {
      const dx = ev.clientX - startRef.current.x;
      setSize(s => Math.min(max, Math.max(min, startRef.current.size + dx)));
    };
    const onUp = () => { window.removeEventListener("pointermove", onMove); window.removeEventListener("pointerup", onUp); };
    window.addEventListener("pointermove", onMove);
    window.addEventListener("pointerup", onUp);
  };
  React.useEffect(() => { if (storageKey) localStorage.setItem(storageKey, size); }, [size, storageKey]);
  return { size, handleProps: { onPointerDown, role: "separator", "aria-orientation": "vertical", tabIndex: 0 } };
}

export function ResizableLayout() {
  const left = useResizable({ initial: 320, min: 200, max: 500, storageKey: "left" });
  return (
    <div style={{ display: "flex", height: "100vh" }}>
      <div style={{ width: left.size }}>Sidebar</div>
      <div {...left.handleProps} style={{ width: 8, cursor: "col-resize", background: "#ddd" }} />
      <div style={{ flex: 1 }}>Main</div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

*2025 variant: ask for `alloy` or `react-resizable-panels` API compatibility.*

**Requirements**
- Show one slide at a time (or 1.2 peeking); arrows wrap infinitely (0 → last, last → 0) with smooth translate.
- Dots indicate active slide; click to jump. Autoplay every 3s, pause on hover/focus and when tab hidden.
- Touch swipe: drag threshold 40px to advance; spring animation.
- Keyboard ←/→, `aria-live` for screen readers.

**Architecture**
- Index state `i`; display via `transform: translateX(-i*100%)` on track `display:flex`.
- Infinite: clone first/last or use modulo math with transition disabled on wrap (`transition: none` then re-enable).
- Autoplay: `useEffect` with `setInterval` that respects `document.hidden` and `isHovered` ref; cleanup on unmount.
- Swipe: `pointerdown/move/up` compute `dx`; if `|dx|>40` commit, else snap back.

**What interviewers look for:** modulo wrap, transition toggle for seamless loop, interval cleanup + visibility, pointer gestures, a11y.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
export function Carousel({ images, autoPlay = 3000 }) {
  const [idx, setIdx] = React.useState(0);
  const [paused, setPaused] = React.useState(false);
  const n = images.length;
  const go = d => setIdx(i => (i + d + n) % n);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    if (paused || document.hidden) return;
    const id = setInterval(() => go(1), autoPlay);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, [paused, autoPlay]);

  const startX = React.useRef(0);
  const onPointerDown = e => startX.current = e.clientX;
  const onPointerUp = e => {
    const dx = e.clientX - startX.current;
    if (Math.abs(dx) > 40) go(dx < 0 ? 1 : -1);
  };

  return (
    <div onMouseEnter={()=>setPaused(true)} onMouseLeave={()=>setPaused(false)}
         onPointerDown={onPointerDown} onPointerUp={onPointerUp}
         role="region" aria-roledescription="carousel" aria-label="Image carousel">
      <div style={{ display: "flex", transform: `translateX(-${idx*100}%)`, transition: "transform 300ms" }}>
        {images.map((src,i)=><img key={i} src={src} style={{ minWidth: "100%" }} alt="" />)}
      </div>
      <button onClick={()=>go(-1)} aria-label="Previous">‹</button>
      <button onClick={()=>go(1)} aria-label="Next">›</button>
      <div role="tablist">{images.map((_,i)=><button key={i} aria-selected={i===idx} onClick={()=>setIdx(i)} />)}</div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

*Trending 2025:* Embla, Swiper, and shadcn carousel all use this pattern; asked to implement from scratch.

**Requirements**
- CRUD todos (add/toggle/edit/delete) with pagination (5 per page), showing page numbers + prev/next + ellipsis.
- Filters: All / Active / Completed; search by text.
- Optimistic updates: UI updates instantly; rollback on simulated API failure (10% chance).
- Persist to localStorage; URL sync `?page=2&filter=active`.

**Architecture**
- State: `todos: {id, text, done}[]`. Derived: `filtered = todos.filter(f)`, `paginated = filtered.slice((page-1)*5, page*5)`.
- Pagination window algorithm: show first, last, current ±2 with `…`.
- Optimistic: `setTodos(prev => [...prev, newTodo]); try { await api.create(newTodo)} catch { setTodos(prev=>prev.filter(t=>t.id!==newTodo.id)) }`.

**What interviewers look for:** derived pagination, window/ellipsis logic, URL sync via `useSearchParams`, optimistic + rollback, empty states.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
function usePagination(items, pageSize=5) {
  const [page, setPage] = React.useState(1);
  const total = Math.ceil(items.length / pageSize);
  const slice = items.slice((page-1)*pageSize, page*pageSize);
  const pages = getPageWindow(page, total);
  return { slice, page, setPage, total, pages };
}

function getPageWindow(cur, tot) {
  if (tot <= 7) return Array.from({length:tot},(_,i)=>i+1);
  const s = new Set([1, tot, cur, cur-1, cur+1]);
  const arr = [...s].filter(n=>n>=1&&n<=tot).sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  return arr.reduce((a,n,i)=> (i&&n-arr[i-1]>1 ? [...a,"…",n] : [...a,n]), []);
}
```

*Why trending:* Pagination + optimistic is the 2025 replacement for plain todo in interviews.

**Requirements**
- Fetch pages (`/api/feed?page=n&limit=20` mock) and append; show skeleton loaders while fetching.
- Sentinel div at bottom observed via `IntersectionObserver` (rootMargin 200px) triggers next fetch.
- Guards: don’t fetch if already loading or no more pages; handle error with Retry.
- Bonus: virtualize after 200 items; scroll restoration.

**Architecture**
- `page` + `items` + `hasMore` + `loading` refs. `observer = new IntersectionObserver(([e])=> e.isIntersecting && !loading && hasMore && loadNext(), {rootMargin:"200px"})`.
- `loading` as ref to avoid stale closure; disconnect on unmount.
- Skeleton: shimmer blocks matching card height to prevent CLS.

**What interviewers look for:** IntersectionObserver vs scroll event, sentinel pattern, in-flight guard, cleanup, error/retry, no duplicate fetch on fast scroll.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
function useInfinite(fetchPage) {
  const [items, setItems] = React.useState([]);
  const [page, setPage] = React.useState(1);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = React.useState(true);
  const loadingRef = React.useRef(false);
  const sentinelRef = React.useRef();

  const load = React.useCallback(async () => {
    if (loadingRef.current || !hasMore) return;
    loadingRef.current = true;
    try {
      const { data, hasMore: hm } = await fetchPage(page);
      setItems(p => [...p, ...data]); setPage(p=>p+1); setHasMore(hm);
    } finally { loadingRef.current = false; }
  }, [page, hasMore]);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    const el = sentinelRef.current; if (!el) return;
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => e.isIntersecting && load(), { rootMargin: "200px" });
    io.observe(el); return () => io.disconnect();
  }, [load]);

  return { items, sentinelRef, hasMore };
}

export function Feed({ fetchPage }) {
  const { items, sentinelRef } = useInfinite(fetchPage);
  return (<>
    {items.map(i => <Card key={i.id} item={i} />)}
    <div ref={sentinelRef} style={{ height: 1 }} />
    <Skeleton count={3} />
  </>);
}
```

*Trending:* Sentinel + skeleton is expected over naive `onscroll` in 2025.

**Requirements**
- Input fetches suggestions from async source debounced 300ms; cancel stale requests (ignore out-of-order responses via sequence id / AbortController).
- Dropdown shows matches with highlighted query substring; keyboard ↑/↓/Enter/Esc, mouse hover, and `aria-activedescendant`.
- Cache results in `Map<query, results>`; handle empty, loading, error states; close on outside click.

**Architecture**
- `useDebouncedValue(query, 300)` + `requestId` ref incremented per fetch; only apply if `id === latest`.
- Highlight: `text.split(new RegExp(`(${escape(query)})`,"i")).map(part => part.toLowerCase()===query.toLowerCase() ? <mark>{part}</mark> : part)`.
- Outside click: `mousedown` listener on document checking `!container.contains(target)`.

**What interviewers look for:** debounce + abort, race handling, caching, highlight, ARIA combobox pattern, outside-click.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
function useDebounce(v, ms=300) { const [d,setD]=React.useState(v); React.useEffect(()=>{const id=setTimeout(()=>setD(v),ms); return ()=>clearTimeout(id);},[v,ms]); return d; }

export function Autocomplete({ fetchSuggest }) {
  const [q, setQ] = React.useState("");
  const dq = useDebounce(q, 300);
  const [items, setItems] = React.useState([]);
  const [active, setActive] = React.useState(-1);
  const seq = React.useRef(0);
  const cache = React.useRef(new Map());

  React.useEffect(() => {
    if (!dq) { setItems([]); return; }
    if (cache.current.has(dq)) { setItems(cache.current.get(dq)); return; }
    const id = ++seq.current;
    fetchSuggest(dq).then(res => {
      if (id !== seq.current) return;
      cache.current.set(dq, res); setItems(res);
    });
  }, [dq]);

  return (
    <div role="combobox" aria-expanded={!!items.length} aria-haspopup="listbox">
      <input value={q} onChange={e=>setQ(e.target.value)} aria-autocomplete="list" aria-activedescendant={active>=0?`opt-${active}`:undefined}
             onKeyDown={e=>{
               if(e.key==="ArrowDown") setActive(a=>Math.min(a+1, items.length-1));
               if(e.key==="ArrowUp") setActive(a=>Math.max(a-1, -1));
               if(e.key==="Enter" && active>=0) setQ(items[active].label);
               if(e.key==="Escape") setItems([]);
             }} />
      <ul role="listbox">{items.map((it,i)=><li key={it.id} id={`opt-${i}`} role="option" aria-selected={i===active} className={i===active?"active":""}>{highlight(it.label, q)}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
  );
}
```

*Trending 2025:* `AbortController` + cache is now the expected answer.

**Requirements**
- Trigger button opens modal via portal; overlay click and Esc close; `aria-modal="true"` + `role="dialog"`.
- Focus trap: Tab / Shift+Tab cycles inside modal; initial focus on first focusable; restore focus to trigger on close.
- Body scroll lock (prevent background scroll) and handle stacked modals (incremental lock count).
- Close animation (fade + scale) before unmount.

**Architecture**
- Portal via `ReactDOM.createPortal(children, document.body)`.
- Focus trap: collect `focusable = modal.querySelectorAll("a,button,input,[tabindex]:not([tabindex='-1'])")`; on `keydown` Tab, wrap.
- Scroll lock: `document.body.style.overflow="hidden"` + padding compensation for scrollbar width; ref-count for stacking.

**What interviewers look for:** portal, trap logic, restore focus ref, scroll lock with compensation, Esc listener cleanup, animation sequencing.

**Reference implementation**
```jsx
export function Modal({ open, onClose, children }) {
  const triggerRef = React.useRef(document.activeElement);
  const modalRef = React.useRef();
  React.useEffect(() => {
    if (!open) return;
    const prev = document.activeElement;
    const focusable = modalRef.current.querySelectorAll("button, [href], input, select, textarea, [tabindex]:not([tabindex='-1'])");
    focusable[0]?.focus();
    const onKey = e => {
      if (e.key === "Escape") onClose();
      if (e.key === "Tab" && focusable.length) {
        const first = focusable[0], last = focusable[focusable.length-1];
        if (e.shiftKey && document.activeElement===first) { e.preventDefault(); last.focus(); }
        else if (!e.shiftKey && document.activeElement===last) { e.preventDefault(); first.focus(); }
      }
    };
    document.addEventListener("keydown", onKey);
    document.body.style.overflow = "hidden";
    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener("keydown", onKey);
      document.body.style.overflow = "";
      (triggerRef.current || prev)?.focus();
    };
  }, [open, onClose]);

  if (!open) return null;
  return ReactDOM.createPortal(
    <div className="overlay" onMouseDown={e=> e.target===e.currentTarget && onClose()} role="presentation">
      <div ref={modalRef} role="dialog" aria-modal="true" className="modal">
        {children}
        <button onClick={onClose} aria-label="Close">×</button>
      </div>
    </div>, document.body);
}
```

